.TH ASK 1
.SH NAME
ask \- put a question to a language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ask
[
.B -ns
] [
.B -m
.I model
] [
.I system
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ask
puts the text on its standard input to a language model, with the
instructions in the file
.IR system ,
and prints the answer.
A tool that wants a model's answer pipes to it, and so does not know
which model it has.
.PP
The model is whatever is mounted at
.BR /mnt/llm :
.I llmsrv
started with the backend, model and key the session chose
.RB ( /lib/ndb/llm ,
read by the login profile), or a model mounted from another machine.
A session is cloned from
.BR new ,
the model written to its
.B model
.RB ( -m ),
the instructions to its
.BR system ,
the question to its
.BR ask ,
the answer read back, and the session closed.
The caller needs no network and no key: the server has them.
.PP
.B $llm
set to anything but
.B llm
is a command instead, given the instructions, a blank line and the
question on its standard input; what it prints is the answer.
Where the command has a word
.BR {system} ,
the instructions are put there as one argument and only the question
goes on its input: the host's Claude command is a plain model as
\f5os claude -p --tools '' --system-prompt {system}\fP.
.PP
.B -n
prints only the model's name
.RB ( unknown
where the server was started with none), and
.B -s
only the settings the question is asked with, one line of
.IB attr = value
words, read from the session's files:
.EX
	temperature=0.70 maxtokens=8192 thinking=disabled
.EE
For a command, each is
.BR unknown :
its settings are its own.
A tool that keeps a record of what a model wrote puts the model's name
and this line beside it.
.SH EXAMPLES
A local model, served by
.IR llmsrv :
.EX
	llmsrv -b openai -u http://localhost:11434/v1
	echo 'Name a prime.' | ask -m llama3.2 /lib/myprompt
.EE
A command's answer, with no model at all, as a test gives one:
.EX
	llm='cat > /dev/null; echo yes'
	echo 'Is it raining?' | ask
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /dis/ask
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR factotum (4);
.BR llmsrv 's
files, in
.B docs/llm-mount.md
in the source tree
.SH BUGS
A command beginning
.B os
runs on the host, and is not confined by the caller's name space.
